WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.344
Your team at fictional Northstar Workshop sees quarterly net income of $84,000 and EBITDA of $180,000.

00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:15.664
A colleague asks whether the difference means the business received another $96,000 in cash.

00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:19.381
Your job is to explain the bridge before drawing a conclusion.

00:00:20.071 --> 00:00:25.405
EBITDA means earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization.

00:00:25.645 --> 00:00:28.077
EBIT stops before the last two adjustments.

00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:33.565
These measures strip selected expenses out of earnings for a particular analytical view.

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:37.261
They do not rewrite the business’s actual costs or bank balance.

00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:42.217
In the SEC reporting context, EBITDA is a non-GAAP measure.

00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:47.236
When used as a performance measure, its reconciliation starts from GAAP net income.

00:00:47.476 --> 00:00:53.300
A calculation with additional adjustments needs a distinct label, such as Adjusted EBITDA.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.102
Before calculating, name the question.

00:00:56.342 --> 00:01:01.590
“How do these specified expenses affect the earnings comparison?” fits this exercise.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:07.654
“Can we spend $180,000 today?” requires cash and obligation information.

00:01:07.894 --> 00:01:11.563
Write one question you would ask before agreeing to a new expenditure.

00:01:12.253 --> 00:01:13.341
Now it's your turn.

00:01:13.581 --> 00:01:16.568
Which response best addresses the colleague’s cash claim?

00:01:16.808 --> 00:01:19.923
